notes:
Maintainer risk: LOW — new path-level CRL signer discovery adds structural anchor reachability without inverting workspace dependency direction. Surface concerns are documentation accuracy (intra-doc link, CHANGELOG migration note) and one parameter (_cert_to_check) that is API surface but no-op. No correctness issues affecting PKITS §4.5 outcomes; all 8 cases verified passing including 3 negative-path discovery tests. Test oracle (NIST PKITS narrative + vectors.json) is independent of code under test.
Confidence: 85% chance of missed bug. Least confident flags: PKIX-lnnl.6 (defense-in-depth gap argument is subtle and relies on the per-entry certificateIssuer walk catching the case implicitly; I could not exhaustively rule out an attacker-crafted bundle that exploits the gap). Uncertainty driven by: (a) I could not run the probe needed to construct a bundle exercising the cert.issuer != crl.issuer + no-§5.3.3-entries case in discovery mode (read-only review constraint); (b) I did not exhaustively walk every code path in find_matching_entry under is_indirect=true with empty crl_entry_extensions, which is the exact safety mechanism PKIX-lnnl.6 relies on; (c) the bundle order semantics (PKIX-lnnl.8) are first-wins by inspection but not exercised by test, so a future refactor could silently change it. Would raise confidence if: (1) a regression test for the PKIX-lnnl.6 scenario, (2) the bundle order tests in PKIX-lnnl.8, (3) the find_parent vs discover_crl_signer alignment in PKIX-lnnl.2.
